====================================================================================================
v133  QLTO ON QCNN AND DisCoCat
====================================================================================================
  V6 needs op(a)op(b) = op(a+b) - a one-parameter abelian group. Every
  Pauli rotation is one, so the gate algebra is never the question. The
  STRUCTURE is: weight sharing (QCNN) and post-selection (DisCoCat).
  TIER A, 32768 shots. Exact reference is tier B (Statevector).

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART 1  QCNN - does the design register resolve SHARED weights?
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
     N   M   occurrences/param   G   circuits   width   cos(V6, exact)
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
     4    6         1-2            1       1        11      +0.9992
     8    9         1-4            1       1        16      +0.9944

   Parameter-shift on the N=8 circuit would be 2M = 18 circuits.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART 2  DisCoCat - does it survive POST-SELECTION on the cups?
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   sentence circuit: 5 qubits, 11 params, cups post-selected on qubits [0, 1, 2, 3]

      G   circuits   width   cos(V6, exact)
   ------------------------------------------------
      1       1        13      +0.9936

   NOTE: this measures the gradient WITHOUT post-selecting the shot record
   - the cups are left as unitaries and the observable is read on the
   sentence wire. That is the honest scope: it shows the design register
   handles the circuit STRUCTURE. Post-selection is a classical filter on
   the same shots and multiplies the shot cost by 1/P(accept), which is
   DisCoCat's own scaling problem and is not measured here.

====================================================================================================
READING IT
====================================================================================================
  QCNN: cos passes at N=4,8 with each parameter appearing at up to 4 sites.
  WEIGHT SHARING IS NOT A PROBLEM, and the reason is structural rather
  than lucky: gi = _pidx[param] is resolved per OCCURRENCE, so every
  site carrying theta_i gets the same column cols[pos[gi]] and hence
  the same sigma_i * R shift. The marginal then decodes d/dtheta_i of
  the whole circuit, which is the chain-rule sum over sites - exactly
  what a shared weight's gradient is.

  G = 1 in both cases, because a QML readout is a single Pauli. So the
  cost is ONE circuit per gradient against parameter-shift's 2M, and with
  readout='qpe' (v132) it stays one circuit whatever the observable.

  THE REAL TENSION FOR QCNN IS NOT FIT, IT IS LEVERAGE. QCNN's whole point
  is O(log N) parameters, and QLTO's advantage is 2M. Cheap encoding and
  large-M leverage pull against each other: at N=8 QCNN the saving is
  2M = 18 circuits (M=9), which is real but far from the 2M at M~2000 of
  a dense ansatz.
  QLTO fits QCNN; QCNN is simply not where QLTO is worth the most.

  SCOPE. N=4,8 QCNN and one 5-qubit DisCoCat sentence, 3 seeds, R=0.25,
  32768 shots, no noise model, no hardware, no training run - this measures
  GRADIENT FIDELITY at a point, not convergence.
